  "narrative_body": "PP fell two seats short of an absolute majority in Andalusia on May 17, and the arithmetic was brutal in its clarity: Juanma Moreno cannot govern Spain's most reliably conservative region without Vox, and neither can Alberto Núñez Feijóo govern Spain.\n\nThe result — PP at 53 seats, Vox at 15, absolute majority requiring 55 in a 109-seat parliament — demolished the notion that PP could build a governing model independent of the far right. Santiago Abascal, Vox's leader, wasted no time declaring his party \"ready to make itself felt\" and calling on Mr Moreno to \"attend to voters.\" How much Mr Moreno pays Vox to form a government in Seville will set the price nationally. PSOE's collapse was equally definitive: 28 seats on 22.7% of the vote — the party's worst result in Andalusia in both seats and share. María Jesús Montero, the regional candidate Pedro Sánchez had deployed as a standard-bearer, will struggle to recover. Mr Sánchez pledged to continue governing until the 2027 general election, invoking his recovery from an earlier Andalusian rout in 2022. The constructive no-confidence mechanism protects him: there is no viable candidate to unseat him.\n\nThe more disruptive result belonged to Adelante Andalucía. José Ignacio García, a 38-year-old teacher and psychologist running a party deliberately separate from the Sumar-IU-Podemos bloc, quadrupled his seats from 2 to 8, outpolling the Sumar-aligned list by over 130,000 votes. Mr García declared that his party had \"removed PP's absolute majority\" and had no interest in joining a Socialist government. The party announced plans to contest general elections nationally — a new fragmentation vector on the left, rooted in the regions and now pointing toward Madrid. Yolanda Díaz was in Beijing for a Chinese government trade summit as the results came in. Ms Díaz's absence from Spain on the night her coalition was eclipsed in its own ground was noted with little sympathy. Sumar's organizational crisis deepens.\n\nEven as the domestic map was reshuffled, Spain was conducting its busiest diplomatic week in months. King Felipe VI's scheduled state visit to Canada (May 19-21) was prepared with unusual deliberateness: the Economy Ministry, not the Foreign Ministry, leads the delegation — a signal that this is economic statecraft, not protocol. The program includes a 170-company business forum at Toronto's MaRS Discovery District, a bilateral AI cooperation agreement linking Indra, Multiverse Computing, and the Barcelona Supercomputing Center to Canadian AI policy, and meetings in Ottawa with Governor General Mary Simon. The bilateral trade base of around €7 billion sits well below what Spanish companies already embedded in Canada — Ferrovial, Acciona, Indra, Grifols — represent as a foundation. Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney's push to diversify away from the United States is the opening Spain is exploiting, and Madrid is framing itself as a preferred partner for states seeking exactly that.\n\nMs Díaz's Beijing visit extended the same logic in a different direction. She met Han Zheng, China's vice president, at the Diaoyutai State Guesthouse, along with senior labor and trade officials, and addressed a summit organized by China's Council for the Promotion of International Trade. She also met Xi Guohua, president of CITIC Group — China's largest state-owned investment conglomerate, with assets exceeding $1 trillion. The visit continued Spain's deliberate policy of deep China engagement irrespective of domestic electoral timing. Mr Sánchez, meanwhile, traveled to Geneva to address the WHO World Health Assembly, building on his bilateral with WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus the previous week. Foreign Minister José Manuel Albares held bilateral meetings with his Kuwaiti and Egyptian counterparts in Madrid and closed an EU-Mercosur implementation seminar in Barcelona — two more threads in Spain's running attempt to position itself as the EU's preferred bridge to the Arab world and Latin America alike.\n\nOn defense, King Felipe VI's visit to the Mando Conjunto del Ciberespacio at the Retamares base on May 14 provided the clearest picture yet of what Spain's NATO contribution looks like in practice. NATO has designated Spain as the host nation for the alliance's Cyber Range capability — a 10,000 m² simulation and training facility for 250 analysts — funded partly by €106 million from NATO's own supply agency. A separate €160 million contract for a cyber-operations platform has gone to Indra. Personnel will triple from 500 to 1,500 by 2030. This is burden-sharing in a domain where Spain's investment does not run into the anti-militarist political constraints that block straightforward spending increases. Defense Minister Margarita Robles attended four sessions of the EU Foreign Affairs Council in its defense format in Brussels that same week — covering Ukraine, the European security strategy, and the Middle East — before appearing before the Congress Defense Committee to account for her participation.\n\nThe US-Spain relationship remained in a quiet plateau. No repair gestures, no new escalation: the confrontation over base access is being managed, not resolved.",
